## Adapt the confirmed exploit (AWPB: exploit + writeup + build)

A working exploit already exists and the environment was built from official
deployment material, so the runtime is well-anchored. PORT that exploit to the
live rebuilt instance — do not re-derive the attack.

{% if instance_data.materials.exploit %}Confirmed exploit under:
{% for path in instance_data.materials.exploit %}- `{{ path }}`
{% endfor %}{% endif %}
- Keep the exploit's approach, target vuln, and its leak → compute → overwrite
  arithmetic intact. Rewriting the approach is out of scope for this route.
- Retarget it at the instance address the harness passes in. Delete every
  hardcoded historical host/port and every absolute address baked in for the
  original environment.
- Resolve every leak at runtime against THIS instance. Recompute libc base,
  stack/heap/PIE bases, and one-gadget/target addresses from the live leak plus
  the rebuilt libc's offsets — never reuse the writeup's or the exploit's
  historical numbers.
- Because B built the runtime, treat it as authoritative: a leak that disagrees
  with the historical exploit almost always means the offset constants must be
  re-derived from the live libc, not that the environment is wrong. Do not
  disable protections the authentic target has to make the old numbers fit.
- Use the writeup only as a diagnostic aid when adaptation stalls — to recover
  the intended primitive, the meaning of a leak, an offset's role, or the
  success signal.
{% if instance_data.materials.writeups %}  Writeup(s): {% for path in instance_data.materials.writeups %}`{{ path }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.
{% endif %}  Do not copy any literal (flag, address, gadget) from it into the solve.
- Prove it: `cage-exploit` must capture the harness-provisioned flag from a
  fresh clean instance. A local run that prints a copied flag is not a pass.
